  "metadata": {
    "title": "Verificabilidade como Fundamento da Soberania Digital: Uma Análise da Convergência Estrutural entre o Projeto LibrePhone (FSF) e o Arkhe OS",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22763818",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-15",
    "description": "<p>The growing social dependence on opaque computational systems has renewed the debate on digital sovereignty:<br>the capacity of individuals and institutions to act independently in the digital environment, without blind trust in<br>vendors. This paper argues that two seemingly distant projects &mdash; LibrePhone, launched by the Free Software<br>Foundation (FSF) in October 2025 to replace proprietary binary blobs in Android devices, and Arkhe OS, a<br>verifiable trust layer for AI agents &mdash; are instances of the same structural principle: converting trust into verifiable<br>evidence. Through comparative documentary analysis, the study maps the isomorphism between the two projects<br>along four dimensions: the trust deficit they attack (sourceless binaries versus opaque stochastic models); the<br>method (clean-room reverse engineering grounded in the Sega v. Accolade precedent versus verification by<br>execution with invariants and falsifiers); the production of auditable public catalogs (a blob database versus model<br>attestation); and license and governance alignment. Three concrete applications are derived for Arkhe OS, with<br>prioritized recommendations. The paper discusses counterarguments &mdash; economic viability of reverse engineering,<br>the distinction between verifying code and verifying stochastic behavior, and the freedom-versus-security<br>tension &mdash; and the methodological limitations of the analysis, concluding that LibrePhone and Arkhe OS form<br>complementary layers of an emerging digital sovereignty stack, from firmware to AI agents.</p>",
